WEBVTT

00:00:00.174 --> 00:00:04.340
Compare total cost of ownership rather than one GPU or API price.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.505
Local cost includes hardware, replacement, power, cooling, storage, support,

00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:09.980
and operator time.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.300
Cloud GPU cost includes running and idle hours, disks, snapshots, and transfer.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.920
Hosted APIs add request or token charges.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.940
Hybrid integration adds gateways, monitoring, testing, and policy maintenance.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.140
Allocate these costs to the workloads that create them.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.280
Measure end-to-end performance from the user.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.540
Local generation may avoid network delay but use a smaller model.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.740
Cloud generation may be faster after arrival yet slower overall because of routing,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.640
upload, queueing, or cold start.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.270
Track time to first output, completion, throughput, error rate, and queue time by route.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.120
Include prompt size and data preparation so comparisons remain meaningful.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.220
Reliability requires understanding dependencies.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.690
Local service depends on owned hardware, power, cooling, and maintenance.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.555
Cloud service depends on network, provider region, account quota, credentials,

00:00:52.555 --> 00:00:54.040
and rate limits.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.075
A failover path must be healthy, compatible, affordable, and authorized for the request.

00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:02.395
Test failover and recovery rather than inferring them from an architecture diagram.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.580
Monitor real demand before scaling either side.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.595
Idle local hardware and unused cloud reservations waste different kinds of money.

00:01:08.595 --> 00:01:12.040
Automatic cloud fallback can create runaway spending during a local outage,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.610
so use budgets, rate limits, queue caps, and alerts.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.060
Optimize the combined service for workload value,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.420
not for maximizing use of either environment.
